Recall the difference formula for tangent:

$$
\tan\left(x - y\right) = \frac{\tan x - \tan y}{1 + \tan x\tan y}
$$

Apply it with $x=150^\circ$ and $y=135^\circ$. The special-angle values are $\tan\left(150^\circ\right)=-\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}$ and $\tan\left(135^\circ\right)=-1$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(15^\circ\right) &= \tan\left(150^\circ - 135^\circ\right) \\
&= \frac{\tan\left(150^\circ\right) - \tan\left(135^\circ\right)}{1 + \tan\left(150^\circ\right)\tan\left(135^\circ\right)} \\
&= \frac{\left(-\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right) - \left(-1\right)}{1 + \left(-\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)\left(-1\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$ to clear the fractions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(15^\circ\right) &= \frac{3\left(\left(-\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right) - \left(-1\right)\right)}{3\left(1 + \left(-\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)\left(-1\right)\right)} \\
&= \frac{3 - \sqrt{3}}{3 + \sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, expand, and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(15^\circ\right) &= \frac{3 - \sqrt{3}}{3 + \sqrt{3}}\cdot \frac{3 - \sqrt{3}}{3 - \sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\left(3 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(3 - \sqrt{3}\right)}{\left(3 + \sqrt{3}\right)\left(3 - \sqrt{3}\right)} \\
&= \frac{12 - 6\sqrt{3}}{6} \\
&= 2 - \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Recall the sum formula for tangent:

$$
\tan\left(x + y\right) = \frac{\tan x + \tan y}{1 - \tan x\tan y}
$$

Apply it with $x=\frac{\pi}{4}$ and $y=\frac{\pi}{6}$. The special-angle values are $\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=1$ and $\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{\pi}{6}\right) \\
&= \frac{\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right) + \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)}{1 - \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)} \\
&= \frac{\left(1\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)}{1 - \left(1\right)\left(\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$ to clear the fractions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \frac{3\left(\left(1\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)\right)}{3\left(1 - \left(1\right)\left(\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{3}\right)\right)} \\
&= \frac{3 + \sqrt{3}}{3 - \sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, expand, and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \frac{3 + \sqrt{3}}{3 - \sqrt{3}}\cdot \frac{3 + \sqrt{3}}{3 + \sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\left(3 + \sqrt{3}\right)\left(3 + \sqrt{3}\right)}{\left(3 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(3 + \sqrt{3}\right)} \\
&= \frac{12 + 6\sqrt{3}}{6} \\
&= 2 + \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Recall the sum formula for tangent:

$$
\tan\left(x + y\right) = \frac{\tan x + \tan y}{1 - \tan x\tan y}
$$

Apply it with $x=240^\circ$ and $y=45^\circ$. The special-angle values are $\tan\left(240^\circ\right)=\sqrt{3}$ and $\tan\left(45^\circ\right)=1$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(285^\circ\right) &= \tan\left(240^\circ + 45^\circ\right) \\
&= \frac{\tan\left(240^\circ\right) + \tan\left(45^\circ\right)}{1 - \tan\left(240^\circ\right)\tan\left(45^\circ\right)} \\
&= \frac{\left(\sqrt{3}\right) + \left(1\right)}{1 - \left(\sqrt{3}\right)\left(1\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Combining the terms gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(285^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, expand, and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(285^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}\cdot \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 + \sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)}{\left(1 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)} \\
&= \frac{4 + 2\sqrt{3}}{-2} \\
&= -2 - \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reordering the two terms gives $-2 - \sqrt{3}=-\sqrt{3} - 2$.

Recall the sum formula for tangent:

$$
\tan\left(x + y\right) = \frac{\tan x + \tan y}{1 - \tan x\tan y}
$$

Apply it with $x=45^\circ$ and $y=60^\circ$. The special-angle values are $\tan\left(45^\circ\right)=1$ and $\tan\left(60^\circ\right)=\sqrt{3}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(105^\circ\right) &= \tan\left(45^\circ + 60^\circ\right) \\
&= \frac{\tan\left(45^\circ\right) + \tan\left(60^\circ\right)}{1 - \tan\left(45^\circ\right)\tan\left(60^\circ\right)} \\
&= \frac{\left(1\right) + \left(\sqrt{3}\right)}{1 - \left(1\right)\left(\sqrt{3}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Combining the terms gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(105^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, expand, and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(105^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}\cdot \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 + \sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)}{\left(1 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)} \\
&= \frac{4 + 2\sqrt{3}}{-2} \\
&= -2 - \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
$$
